{"name":"Oasis Stream","hex":"#a2ebd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oasis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spring of fresh water, surrounded by a fertile region of vegetation, in a desert.","A quiet, peaceful place or situation separated from surrounding noise or bustle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oasis"},{"term":"stream","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small river; a large creek; a body of moving water confined by banks.","All moving waters."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flow in a continuous or steady manner, like a liquid.","To extend; to stretch out with a wavy motion; to float in the wind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stream"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oasis","blocks":[{"text":"An oasis is a fertile area of a desert or semi-desert environment that sustains plant life and provides habitat for animals. Surface water may be present, or water may only be accessible from wells or underground channels created by humans. In geography, an oasis may be a current or past rest stop on a transportation route, or less-than-verdant location that nonetheless provides access to underground water through deep wells created and maintained by humans. Although they depend on a natural condition, such as the presence of water that may be stored in reservoirs and used for irrigation, most oases, as we know them, are artificial."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/ISS-64_Jubba_with_Nefud_Desert%2C_Saudi_Arabia.jpg/330px-ISS-64_Jubba_with_Nefud_Desert%2C_Saudi_Arabia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oasis","wikidata":"Q43742"},{"title":"Stream","blocks":[{"text":"A stream is a continuous body of surface water flowing within the bed and banks of a channel. The channel is a watercourse, although the watercourse may be seasonally or periodically dry. Depending on its location or certain characteristics, a stream may be referred to by a variety of local or regional names. A long, large stream is usually called a river, while a smaller, less voluminous, and more intermittent stream is known as a creek or tributary, among other names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg/330px-%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stream","wikidata":"Q47521"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Taghit_oasis_B%C3%A9char_Algeria.jpg/500px-Taghit_oasis_B%C3%A9char_Algeria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Taghit_oasis_B%C3%A9char_Algeria.jpg/500px-Taghit_oasis_B%C3%A9char_Algeria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Oasis in Taghit, Algeria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATaghit_oasis_B%C3%A9char_Algeria.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Canal_of_irrigation.jpg/500px-Canal_of_irrigation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Canal_of_irrigation.jpg/1280px-Canal_of_irrigation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Canal_of_irrigation.jpg/500px-Canal_of_irrigation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Irrigation canal within the Figuig Oasis in eastern Morocco","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanal_of_irrigation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Oman-Oasis.jpg/500px-Oman-Oasis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p